Where 

- $r(t)$: Reference (desired output).
- $e(t)$: Error signal, defined as $e(s) = r(s) - y(s)$, representing the difference between reference and actual output.
- $u(t)$: Input control signal generated by the controller to drive the system toward the reference.
- $p(t)$: Plant (system to be controlled), characterized by its transfer function $P(s) = y(s)/u(s)$.
- $c(t)$: Controller, with transfer function $C(s)$ that processes the error signal to produce $u(s)$.
- $y(t)$: System output, influenced by both the control input ,disturbances and noises.
- $d(t)$: load disturbance acting at the plant input;
- $n(t)$: measurement noise acting at the plant output;
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
b) Give the expression of $Y(s)$, the Laplace transform of the output signal, as a function of $R(s)$, $D(s)$ and $N(s)$, the Laplace transforms of $r(t)$, $d(t)$ and $n(t)$ respectively.
]

#theorem-box(title:
"From the block diagram described in Question 1, the signals are related as"
)[
$ cases(
  E(s)= R(s)-Y(s),
  U(s)= E(s) C(s),
  Y(s)= N(s) + P(s) [ U(s) + D(s)]
) $ 
]
Substituting $U(s) = C(s)[R(s) - Y(s)]$ into the third equation and collecting
terms in $Y(s)$:
$ Y(s) &= P(s)[C(s) (R(s) - Y(s))+ D(s)]+ N(s) \
  &= P(s)C(s)R(s)- P(s)C(s)Y(s)+P(s)D(s)+N(s)
$
Isolating, and dividing by $1 + P(s)C(s)$ yields the output decomposition:
$ Y(s) [ 1+ P(s)C(s) ] = P(s) C(s) R(s) + P(s) D(s) + N(s) $
$ Y(s)  = frac( P(s) C(s), 1+ P(s)C(s) ) R(s) + frac( P(s) , 1+ P(s)C(s) ) D(s) +frac( 1 , 1+ P(s)C(s) ) N(s) $
= Question 2
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
What do we call the *gang of 4* in control theory? Can you give the general expression and briefly explain what describe the complementary sensitivity function, the load disturbance sensitivity function, and the sensitivity function?
(You may refer to the block diagram of the closed-loop system you drew in Q1.)
]
The *Gang of Four* refers to the four transfer functions that completely
characterise a single-loop feedback system. They are obtained by expressing
both the output $Y(s)$ and the control input $U(s)$ in terms of $R(s)$,
$D(s)$, and $N(s)$.

From Q1, $Y(s)$ is already known. Isolating $U(s)$ in the same way:
$ U(s)&= C(s)[R(s)-Y(s)] \ 
      &= C(s)[R(s)- N(s)-P(s)U(s)-P(s)D(s)] \
      &= C(s)R(s)-C(s) N(s)-P(s)C(s)U(s)-P(s)C(s)D(s)] \
$ 
$
U(s)[1+P(s)C(s)]= C(s)R(s)-C(s)N(s) - P(s)C(s)D(s)
$
#let hb(x, color: blue) = box(baseline: 40%, fill: color.lighten(70%), inset: 8pt, radius: 2pt)[$#x$]
$
U(s)=  hb(frac(C(s), 1+P(s)C(s)),color: #purple)R(s)  - hb(frac(P(s)C(s), 1+P(s)C(s)),color: #green)D(s)- hb(frac(C(s), 1+P(s)C(s)),color: #purple) N(s)
$
$ Y(s)  = hb(frac( P(s) C(s), 1+ P(s)C(s) ),color: #green) R(s) + hb(frac( P(s) , 1+ P(s)C(s) )) D(s) +hb(frac( 1 , 1+ P(s)C(s) ),color: #orange) N(s) $

- Complementary sensitivity function 
$T(s)=hb(frac(P(s)C(s), 1+P(s)C(s)),color: #green)$
- Noise sensitivity function 
$C S(s)=hb(frac(C(s), 1+P(s)C(s)),color: #purple)$
- sensitivity function 
$S(s)= hb(frac( 1 , 1+ P(s)C(s) ),color: #orange) $
- Load sensitivity function
$P S (s) = hb(frac( P(s) , 1+ P(s)C(s) )) $
= Question 3
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
Compute the poles and zeros of the plant transfer function $P(s)$. 
 Can you assess the stability of the system by using only the simplified Nyquist criterion? Why?
]
The linearised plant transfer function is:
    $ P(s) = frac(1,l dot m_c dot s^2 - g dot (m_c + m_p)) $  

*Zeros:* The numerator is constant; $P(s)$ has no finite zeros.

*Poles:* Setting the denominator to zero:
$ l dot m_c dot s^2 - g dot (m_c + m_p) = 0$ 
$ s = plus.minus sqrt(frac(g(m_c+m_p),l dot m_c)) $
where $l = 0.5$ m, $m_c = 1$ kg, $m_p = 0.1$ kg, and $g = 9.81$ m/$s^2$.

#let g = 9.81
#let mc = 1.0
#let mp = 0.1
#let l = 0.5
#let s_val = calc.sqrt(g * (mc + mp) / (l * mc))
// too lazy to compute , just ask nicely typst
$ s approx plus.minus #calc.round(s_val, digits: 4) $

The simplified (gain-based) Nyquist criterion applies only to *open-loop stable*
plants. Since $P(s)$ has a pole at $s = +#calc.round(s_val, digits: 4)$ rad/s
in the open right half-plane, the simplified criterion *cannot* be used; the
full Nyquist criterion must be applied instead.
= Question 4
#quote-box[
#set text(fill: black)
Without any control, is the system stable ? Justify your answer.
Below are the Bode and Nyquist plots of $P(s)$. It also shows how the output evolves in open-loop when the system experiences disturbance $d(t)$.
]
The uncontrolled plant is *unstable*. The right half-plane pole at
$s = +#calc.round(s_val, digits: 4)$ rad/s means that any infinitesimal
perturbation causes the state to diverge exponentially. @fig-openloop confirms
this across all four panels: the Bode magnitude grows unboundedly at low
frequencies with no 0 dB crossover from above; 
the Nyquist curve lies entirely on the negative real axis between $-0.0927$
and $0$; since $P(s)$ has an open-loop RHP pole, the full Nyquist criterion
must be applied and no conclusion can be drawn from encirclement of $-1+0j$
alone;
finally, the
time response to $d(t) = sin(4 pi t)$ shows the pole angle $theta(t)$
diverging exponentially, consistent with the poles at
$s approx plus.minus #calc.round(s_val, digits: 3)$ rad/s.
#figure(
  image("q4.svg", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    Open-loop analysis of the uncontrolled cart-pole.
  ]
) <fig-openloop>
#let tau  = 0.02          // delay tolerance [s]
